WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.990
I feel like today, people like myself,

00:00:01.990 --> 00:00:04.110
we have actually the means
and the power to fight.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.570
And there has never
been a time in the world

00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:07.510
actually like that until
bitcoin came in 2008.

00:00:08.690 --> 00:00:10.240
Everything is possible actually,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:11.350
because money is everything.

00:00:11.350 --> 00:00:13.040
When Fodé Diop was a high school

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:14.580
senior growing up in Senegal,

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.810
he was accepted to Emporia
State University in Kansas

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:20.360
to study engineering
and to play basketball.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:23.090
But before enrolling, the
value of the tuition money

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:26.780
his father had set aside
was cut in half overnight

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:28.270
because of a backroom deal

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:30.723
spearheaded by the
International Monetary Fund

00:00:30.723 --> 00:00:32.165
and the French government.

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:34.270
So all the money my dad saved for me

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:35.480
to basically to go to college,

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.520
overnight was worth 50 percent less.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:38.890
That experience is what led

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.820
to Diop's current career
as a software developer,

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:43.999
working to make bitcoin easy to use

00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:47.420
as a medium of exchange
for anyone in Senegal

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.676
with a smartphone.

00:00:48.676 --> 00:00:50.270
Bitcoin is not controlled by anybody.

00:00:50.270 --> 00:00:51.320
Nobody can come overnight

00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:52.790
and say that now it's worth this much

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:54.590
or 50 percent less or 40 percent less.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:55.900
So if you are out in a country where

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.220
maybe your money might
be divided on a whim

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:00.900
of other nations interfering
with the local economy,

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.520
storing your money in Bitcoin
might be a better deal

00:01:03.520 --> 00:01:04.720
for you in the future

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.120
to preserve your wealth
and preserve your money.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:09.500
So it's providing a parallel stored value

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.370
and a superior one that is
not going to be the whim

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:13.410
of the French government,

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:14.418
the U.S. government, the
Senegalese government.

00:01:14.418 --> 00:01:15.251
That's right.

00:01:15.251 --> 00:01:18.104
Senegal has been subject
to monetary colonialism

00:01:18.104 --> 00:01:20.110
since 1945.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.970
When France ratified the
Bretton Woods Agreement,

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:24.572
which gave it control over the currency

00:01:24.572 --> 00:01:27.377
of 14 African nations.

00:01:27.377 --> 00:01:30.802
On the morning of January 11th, 1994,

00:01:30.802 --> 00:01:34.870
the CFA Franc or seefa was
pegged to the French Franc

00:01:34.870 --> 00:01:37.080
at a evaluation of 1 to 50.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.540
The following day, it was cut to 1 to 100,

00:01:40.540 --> 00:01:42.700
after the French government
gave into pressure

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.430
from the International
Monetary Fund and World Bank

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:47.170
to devalue the currency.

00:01:47.170 --> 00:01:50.560
Not only did this temporarily
crushed Diop's college dreams,

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.170
it also led to widespread political unrest

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:55.610
as West African subsistence farmers

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:58.640
could no longer afford to
import essential goods,

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:00.930
including drugs to treat malaria.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:02.920
It was really a big, big uproar

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:04.240
for these African countries,

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:06.098
but the local people
really found themselves

00:02:06.098 --> 00:02:09.480
pretty much robbed because
your purchasing power

00:02:09.480 --> 00:02:10.637
overnight was worth half.

00:02:10.637 --> 00:02:11.970
"The cruel irony was that the economic
fate of millions of Senegalese

00:02:14.270 --> 00:02:16.530
was completely out of their own hands,"

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:19.697
writes The Human Rights
Foundation's, Alex Gladstine,

00:02:19.697 --> 00:02:22.080
"No amount of protests
could overthrow their

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:23.500
economic masters."

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.260
I went to the embassy, I
got rejected, they say,

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:27.564
actually I did not have enough money.

00:02:27.564 --> 00:02:29.227
And I was really heartbroken.

00:02:29.227 --> 00:02:31.070
Diop's father was eventually able

00:02:31.070 --> 00:02:32.257
to pull together the money

00:02:32.257 --> 00:02:34.650
and Diop got to attend college after all.

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:36.700
After graduating, he came across a video

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:39.390
featuring a Senegalese
scientist and historian

00:02:39.390 --> 00:02:42.539
who criticized the French
African monetary system.

00:02:42.539 --> 00:02:44.757
At this time, I wasn't really conscious

00:02:44.757 --> 00:02:46.470
about what really happened.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:47.580
Diop says it was the first

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:51.179
of three awakenings that set
him on his current career path.

00:02:51.179 --> 00:02:53.350
Every once in a while,

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:55.254
a revolutionary product comes along

00:02:55.254 --> 00:02:58.040
that changes everything.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:02:59.520
The next was Steve Jobs'

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:02.777
famous 2007 announcement of the iPhone,

00:03:02.777 --> 00:03:05.300
which ultimately led him to consider

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:09.280
how poorly traditional banks
serve the citizens of Senegal.

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:11.933
Every where you go in downtown
is a bank on every corner.

00:03:11.933 --> 00:03:13.675
Bank of Africa, Bank of this,

00:03:13.675 --> 00:03:15.722
Bank of that, bank of whatever.

00:03:15.722 --> 00:03:18.060
And for me, I can't help but wonder,

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:19.820
with so many banks here,

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:22.271
how can we have the lowest
number of banked people

00:03:22.271 --> 00:03:23.149
in the world?

00:03:23.149 --> 00:03:25.160
Who are these banks serving?

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.185
The people who actually
live in these remote areas

00:03:27.185 --> 00:03:30.110
have to pretty much travel
about two to four hours,

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:31.540
just to go inside a bank.

00:03:31.540 --> 00:03:32.870
Jobs' handheld computer

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:34.840
created an opportunity to circumvent

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:36.830
these institutions all together.

00:03:36.830 --> 00:03:39.630
My vision of the future is
that ultimately the bank,

00:03:39.630 --> 00:03:41.330
your bank account, is going to become

00:03:41.330 --> 00:03:43.190
pretty much through
your cell phone device.

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:44.570
But Jobs' revolutionary

00:03:44.570 --> 00:03:46.200
new communication device

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.420
needed a decentralized
monetary system to match,

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.190
which is where Bitcoin comes in.

00:03:51.190 --> 00:03:54.803
Reading Satoshi Nakamoto's
groundbreaking 2008 paper

00:03:54.803 --> 00:03:57.100
led to Joab's third awakening.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:03:58.300
When I read the white paper.

00:03:58.300 --> 00:03:59.970
I said, okay, well this is a weapon

00:03:59.970 --> 00:04:01.220
for us to fight oppression.

00:04:01.220 --> 00:04:03.010
But the Bitcoin network alone

00:04:03.010 --> 00:04:05.940
isn't well-suited to
becoming a medium of exchange

00:04:05.940 --> 00:04:09.322
for everyday purchases in
Senegal or anywhere else.

00:04:09.322 --> 00:04:12.250
That's because it
currently has the capacity

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:15.532
to process only a handful
of transactions per second,

00:04:15.532 --> 00:04:19.040
which is where the Bitcoin
Lightning Network comes in.

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:22.020
So Bitcoin, for it to
rival the likes of Visa

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:24.250
and MasterCard, it
would have to do upwards

00:04:24.250 --> 00:04:26.110
of like 20,000 transactions per second,

00:04:26.110 --> 00:04:28.220
or like even 25,000.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:29.443
Today unfortunately Bitcoin only does

00:04:29.443 --> 00:04:32.490
7 to about 12 transactions per second.

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:33.880
Lightning Network works like a bar tab.

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.340
When you go to the bar,

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.600
you open up a tab and you
pretty much go back and forth

00:04:37.600 --> 00:04:39.357
and take as many transactions as you want.

00:04:39.357 --> 00:04:40.940
And then you settle your
bill at the end of the night.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:42.375
So Bitcoin is a base layer,

00:04:42.375 --> 00:04:45.810
but unfortunately it's not
really fast or nor is it cheap.

00:04:45.810 --> 00:04:47.620
So basically what happens is you and I,

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.880
we opened up a channel or like
a Lightning Network channel.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:53.460
Basically we put some
funds in there that we lock

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:55.700
and then we protect as many as we want,

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:56.533
millions of times,

00:04:56.533 --> 00:04:57.820
basically at the end of the month,

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:00.610
we basically go settle on
the Bitcoin layer itself,

00:05:00.610 --> 00:05:02.080
which is a little bit more expensive

00:05:02.080 --> 00:05:03.594
because of course it's the base layer,

00:05:03.594 --> 00:05:04.724
but lightening wise,

00:05:04.724 --> 00:05:07.441
our transactions will
pretty much cost a fraction

00:05:07.441 --> 00:05:08.290
of a penny.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:10.739
So it's almost instant and almost free.

00:05:10.739 --> 00:05:12.170
And then at the point,

00:05:12.170 --> 00:05:14.830
we can rival the MasterCard
and the Visas of the world.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:15.770
With Bitcoin,

00:05:15.770 --> 00:05:18.443
the Lightning Network, and
ubiquitous smartphones,

00:05:18.443 --> 00:05:21.800
Diop thinks it's possible
to build a new free market

00:05:21.800 --> 00:05:24.902
monetary system that will
finally liberate Senegal

00:05:24.902 --> 00:05:26.778
and other African countries

00:05:26.778 --> 00:05:29.634
from the last vestiges of colonialism.

00:05:29.634 --> 00:05:32.570
I feel like maybe governments
are still threatened maybe,

00:05:32.570 --> 00:05:35.610
but to me, open source
technology is just beautiful.

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:37.163
And I believe actually that
our work is so important

00:05:37.163 --> 00:05:39.960
that we have to do it no matter
what the consequences are.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:41.450
So if we can leverage this technology

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:42.900
to fight the oppressor, right,

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:44.020
and technology, open source technology

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:45.319
is actually what we need right now.

00:05:45.319 --> 00:05:46.578
And it's here.

00:05:46.578 --> 00:05:48.245
So why not use it?

